# Customer Segmentation Using K-Means and DBSCAN

## Project Overview

This project applies unsupervised machine learning techniques to segment mall customers based on their annual income and spending scores.

The project was completed as part of the **Elevvo Pathways Machine Learning Track – Task 2: Customer Segmentation**.

The primary objective is to identify groups of customers with similar characteristics to help businesses understand customer behaviour and develop targeted marketing strategies.

## Objectives

- Explore and understand customer data.
- Perform data preprocessing and feature scaling.
- Apply K-Means clustering to segment customers.
- Determine the optimal number of clusters using the Elbow Method and Silhouette Analysis.
- Visualise customer segments.
- Experiment with DBSCAN as an alternative clustering algorithm.
- Compare the performance of both clustering approaches.

## Dataset

The project uses the Mall Customer Segmentation dataset from Kaggle.

**Dataset source:** https://www.kaggle.com/datasets/vjchoudhary7/customer-segmentation-tutorial-in-python

The dataset contains 200 customer records and five attributes:

- CustomerID
- Genre
- Age
- Annual Income (k$)
- Spending Score (1-100)

For the primary clustering analysis, Annual Income and Spending Score were selected as the features.

## Technologies and Libraries

- Python
- Google Colab
- Pandas
- NumPy
- Matplotlib
- Seaborn
- Scikit-learn

## Methodology

### 1. Data Exploration and Preprocessing

The dataset was loaded and examined to understand its structure, data types and statistical characteristics. Missing values and duplicate records were also checked.

### 2. Feature Selection and Scaling

Annual Income and Spending Score were selected for clustering.

StandardScaler was applied to standardise the features before clustering.

### 3. K-Means Clustering

The Elbow Method and Silhouette Analysis were used to determine the optimal number of clusters.

The analysis supported selecting five clusters (K = 5).

### 4. Cluster Visualisation

Scatter plots were created to visualise the five customer segments. Cluster centroids were also plotted to show the centre of each segment.

### 5. DBSCAN Clustering

DBSCAN was implemented as an alternative density-based clustering algorithm.

The model was configured with:
- eps = 0.3
- min_samples = 5

### 6. Algorithm Evaluation

The Silhouette Score was used to evaluate cluster separation and compare the clustering approaches.

## Results

### K-Means Results

- Number of clusters: 5
- Silhouette Score: 0.5547
- Total customers: 200

The five customer segments revealed different income and spending patterns.

| Cluster | Average Annual Income (k$) | Average Spending Score |
|---|---:|---:|
| 0 | 55.30 | 49.52 |
| 1 | 86.54 | 82.13 |
| 2 | 25.73 | 79.36 |
| 3 | 88.20 | 17.11 |
| 4 | 26.30 | 20.91 |

### DBSCAN Results

- Number of clusters: 7
- Silhouette Score: 0.5243
- Noise points: 35

DBSCAN identified seven clusters and classified 35 customers as noise points.

### Algorithm Comparison

| Metric | K-Means | DBSCAN |
|---|---:|---:|
| Number of clusters | 5 | 7 |
| Silhouette Score | 0.5547 | 0.5243 |
| Noise points | 0 | 35 |

**Note:** The K-Means silhouette score was calculated using all 200 customers, while the DBSCAN score was calculated using only the 165 non-noise customers. Therefore, the scores are not a perfectly like-for-like comparison.

## Conclusion

The project demonstrates how unsupervised machine learning can be used to identify meaningful customer segments.

K-Means produced five interpretable customer groups and achieved a silhouette score of 0.5547. DBSCAN provided an alternative approach by identifying density-based groups and detecting observations that did not belong to sufficiently dense regions.

Based on the results, K-Means is a reasonable choice for this particular segmentation task because it assigns all customers to five interpretable groups.

The findings can help businesses understand customer behaviour and develop more targeted marketing strategies.

## Project Files

- `Customer_Segmentation_KMeans.ipynb` – Google Colab notebook containing the implementation and analysis.
- `Mall_Customers.csv` – Original dataset.
- `Customer_Segmentation_Results.csv` – Customer dataset containing the K-Means cluster assignments.
- `README.md` – Project documentation.

